1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,010
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:01,361 --> 00:00:02,486
I am Reza Amini.

3
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:05,325
I'm a senior engineer and
the founder of Flashback.

4
00:00:05,967 --> 00:00:09,658
thanks to the Conf42 team for
having me, and thanks to everyone watching

5
00:00:10,658 --> 00:00:15,598
So my talk today is called Your Agent
Failed Mid-Run, Logs Won't Replay It.

6
00:00:16,111 --> 00:00:16,919
let's get to it.

7
00:00:18,169 --> 00:00:19,384
Here's the plan for today.

8
00:00:19,821 --> 00:00:24,204
We will talk about debugging AI
agents in general, and we will debug

9
00:00:24,209 --> 00:00:26,194
an AI agent that made a mistake.

10
00:00:26,696 --> 00:00:30,590
Basically, the agent reserved the
wrong amount of stock in the warehouse.

11
00:00:30,751 --> 00:00:36,008
The customer ordered three cartons, and
the agent reserved three pallets instead.

12
00:00:36,856 --> 00:00:39,600
I saved every model and
tool calls from that run.

13
00:00:39,727 --> 00:00:44,574
we will replay it on my laptop offline
for free, find the step that went wrong,

14
00:00:45,105 --> 00:00:47,257
fix it, and then check the fix together.

15
00:00:47,692 --> 00:00:51,188
And we will do all that without
running the whole agent live again.

16
00:00:52,981 --> 00:00:57,529
But first, before going into the details,
why is debugging an AI agent hard?

17
00:00:58,181 --> 00:01:02,346
Let's start with how we would
normally fix and debug normal or

18
00:01:02,346 --> 00:01:06,542
traditional softwares and what's,
different when it's an AI agent.

19
00:01:07,329 --> 00:01:11,001
With normal or traditional
software, we know the steps.

20
00:01:11,043 --> 00:01:15,433
We reproduce the bug on our machine,
change something, test it, and

21
00:01:15,434 --> 00:01:16,956
then we would eventually ship it.

22
00:01:16,975 --> 00:01:21,392
And we can, reproduce these steps
over and over again without any

23
00:01:21,393 --> 00:01:23,776
limitation in an isolated environment.

24
00:01:24,469 --> 00:01:25,836
An AI agent is different.

25
00:01:25,877 --> 00:01:30,604
It asks a model what to do, then
it calls tools like APIs and

26
00:01:30,604 --> 00:01:32,697
databases, to take an action.

27
00:01:33,175 --> 00:01:37,460
And say an agent, it fails in
middle of a run in production.

28
00:01:37,874 --> 00:01:42,039
Our go-to is always logs and
traces to tell us what happened,

29
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:47,856
but that can't play back the run
on our machine and, test the fix.

30
00:01:48,374 --> 00:01:53,026
So most of us run the whole agent
again, and that have consequences.

31
00:01:53,061 --> 00:01:58,972
That cost more model usage, it calls
every live system again, and it would…

32
00:01:59,319 --> 00:02:02,978
it might even give you a different
result because of the nature

33
00:02:03,003 --> 00:02:05,048
of LLMs and agents in general.

34
00:02:05,641 --> 00:02:11,756
So this talk is about bringing those same
debugging habits and steps to AI agents

35
00:02:12,756 --> 00:02:14,537
So let me walk you through an example.

36
00:02:14,711 --> 00:02:17,048
Let's assume an AI agent for a warehouse.

37
00:02:17,489 --> 00:02:21,764
It's a made-up example, but the method
works for any agent in any domain.

38
00:02:21,776 --> 00:02:22,902
It doesn't really matter.

39
00:02:23,439 --> 00:02:26,335
In this particular case,
the agent does five steps.

40
00:02:26,771 --> 00:02:29,634
It, looks up the customer
information in step one.

41
00:02:29,646 --> 00:02:32,821
In step two, reads the order
and the stock availability.

42
00:02:33,075 --> 00:02:35,432
In step three, it decides what can ship.

43
00:02:35,905 --> 00:02:38,332
In step four, send warehouse request.

44
00:02:38,651 --> 00:02:39,569
And step five…

45
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:43,964
And in the step five, it finally,
draft a reply for the customer.

46
00:02:45,134 --> 00:02:47,919
So now let's follow one
order through the agent.

47
00:02:48,443 --> 00:02:52,920
let's say a new order comes in and
a customer orders three cartons.

48
00:02:53,373 --> 00:02:58,584
similarly, the agent, look up the customer
information, read the order, and the

49
00:02:58,587 --> 00:03:01,238
model decides, three cartons can ship.

50
00:03:01,526 --> 00:03:03,430
So far so good until step three.

51
00:03:04,045 --> 00:03:08,399
But in step four, which is the act,
the agent sends the warehouse a request

52
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:10,852
for three pallets, not three cartons.

53
00:03:11,386 --> 00:03:15,442
so the warehouse tools accepted it,
and, in this particular case, nothing

54
00:03:15,443 --> 00:03:19,829
crashed and every code worked, but
the agent reserved the, wrong stock.

55
00:03:20,324 --> 00:03:24,919
so our go-to in this case
would be looking at logs.

56
00:03:25,135 --> 00:03:28,879
So what do the logs tell us in this case?

57
00:03:29,063 --> 00:03:33,095
These are good logs with one line
for each step that the agent took.

58
00:03:33,509 --> 00:03:38,749
so in this case, the warehouse calls,
say success, but that only means the

59
00:03:38,779 --> 00:03:42,498
tool accepted the request, and it
doesn't mean the request was right.

60
00:03:43,162 --> 00:03:44,881
so now let's look at the units.

61
00:03:45,101 --> 00:03:50,549
In, in the order unit, which is the second
line, the order says, three cartons.

62
00:03:51,062 --> 00:03:55,854
And similarly, the model correctly
decided, three cartons as well.

63
00:03:56,319 --> 00:03:59,449
but the request says
suddenly three pallets.

64
00:03:59,792 --> 00:04:03,766
So, so in this case, the unit changed
when the agent built the request

65
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:05,784
before it went to the warehouse.

66
00:04:06,375 --> 00:04:10,061
It's worth mentioning that, to
get this information, it really

67
00:04:10,071 --> 00:04:14,700
depends how structured and
extensive your logging system is.

68
00:04:15,205 --> 00:04:18,179
And in this case, these
logs are short and clean.

69
00:04:18,192 --> 00:04:22,964
But in real, logs in production, these
lines would be buried among lots of

70
00:04:22,991 --> 00:04:25,524
others, so they are much harder to find.

71
00:04:26,253 --> 00:04:30,280
And, even when the logs show,
where it went wrong in this,

72
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:32,147
in this case, we still need…

73
00:04:32,589 --> 00:04:36,047
We still need to, test the fix with
the same inputs, and that's the

74
00:04:36,050 --> 00:04:41,200
hard part So the question might be,
why not just run the agent again?

75
00:04:41,214 --> 00:04:45,331
And the answer to that question is
that it isn't the same test anymore.

76
00:04:45,769 --> 00:04:48,827
Let's assume we wanna do a
fresh run with the same order.

77
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:49,616
What would happen?

78
00:04:50,323 --> 00:04:52,970
so step one calls the
customer system again.

79
00:04:53,029 --> 00:04:58,274
In this case, step two reads the stock
again, and the stock, right now, might

80
00:04:58,284 --> 00:05:02,142
include the reservation from the first
run, so it might give us a wrong counter.

81
00:05:02,856 --> 00:05:07,498
Step three ask the-- asks the model
again, and it might decide differently.

82
00:05:07,813 --> 00:05:10,886
So by step four, the inputs
may have changed already.

83
00:05:11,804 --> 00:05:16,425
maybe in this fresh run, this time
the request says cartons and the

84
00:05:16,427 --> 00:05:18,352
box-- and the bug looks fixed.

85
00:05:18,647 --> 00:05:23,607
Maybe it says pallets again, and the
warehouse gets another duplicated request.

86
00:05:24,152 --> 00:05:28,000
So either way, we paid for more
model calls, and we haven't

87
00:05:28,053 --> 00:05:29,718
really tested a fix yet.

88
00:05:30,187 --> 00:05:34,318
And obviously, in this particular
case, it has a lot of side effects.

89
00:05:34,332 --> 00:05:40,092
It means that the warehouse is getting
a duplicate, order potentially, or we

90
00:05:40,092 --> 00:05:45,295
might, get a wrong, stock availability
counter or mistakenly update the

91
00:05:45,295 --> 00:05:47,288
counter, which we do not want.

92
00:05:47,293 --> 00:05:50,634
So it might have a lot of side
effect, side effects running

93
00:05:50,635 --> 00:05:52,099
the entire agent live again.

94
00:05:52,825 --> 00:05:58,527
And even if we change a fix or,
or change something and run it all

95
00:05:58,527 --> 00:06:00,314
over again, it's the same situation.

96
00:06:01,677 --> 00:06:08,157
If the results changes, was it
our fix or was just a model, and

97
00:06:08,164 --> 00:06:09,948
the agent took a different path?

98
00:06:10,301 --> 00:06:11,294
We can't really tell

99
00:06:12,294 --> 00:06:17,362
So here is a different habit and approach
in three steps: record, replay, fork.

100
00:06:17,876 --> 00:06:22,939
it lets us debug an agent the way we
debug normal or traditional softwares.

101
00:06:23,941 --> 00:06:29,825
Record mean that while the agent runs, you
save each model and, each tool call, what

102
00:06:29,862 --> 00:06:32,923
went in, what came out, to a local file.

103
00:06:33,186 --> 00:06:36,917
I call that file a cassette
because, you can play it back.

104
00:06:37,438 --> 00:06:42,744
replay means you run the agent again
on your machine exactly, as how it went

105
00:06:42,745 --> 00:06:48,035
through the last time, but each model and
tool call gets its saved results from the

106
00:06:48,236 --> 00:06:50,217
file instead of calling the real thing.

107
00:06:51,093 --> 00:06:54,298
And fork is similar to the branch in Git.

108
00:06:54,535 --> 00:06:57,746
Means you pick the call where
things went wrong, change

109
00:06:57,762 --> 00:07:00,064
something, and, run live from there.

110
00:07:00,365 --> 00:07:04,330
Everything before the fork or before
that point come from the file.

111
00:07:05,141 --> 00:07:06,607
so let's go through each one.

112
00:07:07,607 --> 00:07:09,005
Let's start with the recording.

113
00:07:09,283 --> 00:07:11,985
That's just saving the
model and tool calls.

114
00:07:12,698 --> 00:07:17,682
You record at the points where your
agent calls a model or a tool, and that's

115
00:07:17,682 --> 00:07:19,412
done by adding a few lines of code.

116
00:07:19,875 --> 00:07:24,561
For each call, we save the input,
the output, any error, how long

117
00:07:24,562 --> 00:07:26,284
it took, and et cetera in order.

118
00:07:26,625 --> 00:07:31,144
In our agent, the demo we talked about
earlier, the five steps become six

119
00:07:31,144 --> 00:07:37,415
calls because, generally agents in
production, every step can have multiple

120
00:07:37,658 --> 00:07:39,514
calls being called at the same time.

121
00:07:39,762 --> 00:07:44,097
But for simplicity, only step four
in this particular case is making

122
00:07:44,099 --> 00:07:47,940
two separate calls, and the rest
of these steps are making a single

123
00:07:47,987 --> 00:07:50,021
call, and we can record all of that.

124
00:07:50,697 --> 00:07:55,013
So in our case, step one and two
are tool calls that read data.

125
00:07:55,347 --> 00:08:01,024
step three is a model call that decides
what to ship, and step four is two calls.

126
00:08:01,273 --> 00:08:04,796
one is the model writes, the
warehouse request, and the

127
00:08:04,798 --> 00:08:06,826
other is a tool that sends it.

128
00:08:07,194 --> 00:08:10,809
And step five is a model
call for the reply.

129
00:08:11,068 --> 00:08:15,007
So in total, six calls,
our agent, would make.

130
00:08:15,573 --> 00:08:20,167
so in our example, in step three, the
model decides, three cartons, which

131
00:08:20,167 --> 00:08:21,888
is correct, and that's the decision.

132
00:08:22,170 --> 00:08:25,820
In call four, the model writes
the warehouse request, and

133
00:08:25,833 --> 00:08:29,602
it says three pallets in this
case, and that's the proposal.

134
00:08:30,078 --> 00:08:35,664
to keep the demo simple, I added a rule
that makes the model gets the units wrong

135
00:08:35,687 --> 00:08:38,591
on purpose, so we have a mistake to debug.

136
00:08:38,920 --> 00:08:42,442
But the real bug is in the code
which we will get into later,

137
00:08:42,519 --> 00:08:46,862
which it sends the proposal to the
warehouse where it should send s--

138
00:08:47,036 --> 00:08:48,438
where it should send the decision.

139
00:08:49,634 --> 00:08:53,230
also worth mentioning that
adding recording doesn't really

140
00:08:53,235 --> 00:08:54,912
change how your agent works.

141
00:08:55,284 --> 00:08:59,389
It only saves the calls you decide
to save, and you wrap them in code.

142
00:09:00,051 --> 00:09:03,647
and also worth mentioning
that recording doesn't mean a

143
00:09:03,647 --> 00:09:05,434
middleman and doesn't mean proxy.

144
00:09:05,434 --> 00:09:09,324
You don't need to deal with
any of that And next is replay.

145
00:09:09,787 --> 00:09:13,653
So now we record and we have the
file which we called cassette,

146
00:09:14,064 --> 00:09:18,257
and now we use that saved results
and don't make any live calls.

147
00:09:18,957 --> 00:09:24,167
So, basically replays, run the
agent again locally for free

148
00:09:24,542 --> 00:09:26,566
so you can see what it did.

149
00:09:27,014 --> 00:09:30,871
when the agent makes a model or
tool call, it gets the results

150
00:09:30,871 --> 00:09:32,686
from the file in this case.

151
00:09:33,045 --> 00:09:36,991
So the model isn't asked again, and
the tools aren't really called again.

152
00:09:37,761 --> 00:09:40,832
so replays run on your machine,
so you don't need to upload

153
00:09:40,834 --> 00:09:45,107
anything or deal with any cloud
systems or, anything in that sort.

154
00:09:46,062 --> 00:09:51,721
so in this particular case, if we replay
the agent locally on our machine, the

155
00:09:51,727 --> 00:09:55,702
warehouse doesn't, get a second request,
so it doesn't have any side effects.

156
00:09:56,398 --> 00:10:02,658
And you can replay the failed, failed run
in this case as, many times as you would

157
00:10:02,659 --> 00:10:04,790
like without paying for those calls again.

158
00:10:05,790 --> 00:10:08,052
and every replay shows the same thing.

159
00:10:08,052 --> 00:10:13,331
So in our case, if we do the replay,
no matter how many times, the order

160
00:10:13,346 --> 00:10:17,776
would say cartons, the decision
would say cartons by the, but the

161
00:10:17,788 --> 00:10:20,486
request would say pallets in our case.

162
00:10:21,251 --> 00:10:25,229
so there is one limit here that I would
like to point out, and it matters.

163
00:10:25,294 --> 00:10:30,652
replay only covers the calls you recorded,
so the rest of the code still runs.

164
00:10:31,219 --> 00:10:35,600
if it reads the clock, for instance,
use random numbers, keep hidden

165
00:10:35,602 --> 00:10:40,445
stats, or, talk to anything outside
of the calls that you recorded,

166
00:10:40,445 --> 00:10:42,705
the, the run can still, change.

167
00:10:43,296 --> 00:10:46,977
and if the agent, let's say, asks
for something different from what

168
00:10:47,009 --> 00:10:51,842
was saved prev-previously, replay
stops at that call and tells you.

169
00:10:52,152 --> 00:10:56,290
And that's a positive thing which later on
we will see and test it in, in the code.

170
00:10:56,832 --> 00:11:00,183
so it doesn't really just
carry on, with the changes.

171
00:11:00,184 --> 00:11:03,061
So th-those are the important
points to, keep in mind.

172
00:11:03,849 --> 00:11:04,563
So now let…

173
00:11:04,616 --> 00:11:09,586
now let's talk about the fork, where
this is where, we make the fix happen.

174
00:11:10,228 --> 00:11:16,332
So in our case, we fork from call four
because, call three was the agent making

175
00:11:16,332 --> 00:11:21,133
a decision of three carton, which was
correct, and from call four onwards, the

176
00:11:21,133 --> 00:11:23,614
unit changed to pallets, which was wrong.

177
00:11:24,347 --> 00:11:28,158
so in this case, call one to
three comes from the recording,

178
00:11:28,166 --> 00:11:29,917
so they don't run again.

179
00:11:30,233 --> 00:11:33,445
From call four onwards,
everything runs live.

180
00:11:33,478 --> 00:11:37,866
The model answers again, the warehouse
gets a new request, and the model

181
00:11:37,868 --> 00:11:43,333
writes a new reply back to the customer
So as I mentioned, so in this case,

182
00:11:43,352 --> 00:11:48,102
we fork, but the fix is in the code,
which in this case, we will see it

183
00:11:48,102 --> 00:11:53,224
later on in the actual, example in
the code, which it needed to send the

184
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:55,175
decision rather than the proposal.

185
00:11:55,613 --> 00:11:59,063
and as I mentioned, the calls
from one to three are exactly,

186
00:11:59,408 --> 00:12:01,131
same as the failure run.

187
00:12:01,497 --> 00:12:05,336
So if something changes from
our fork, it's not because

188
00:12:05,336 --> 00:12:06,881
of the earlier steps anymore.

189
00:12:07,436 --> 00:12:11,542
And it's worth mentioning it
once more that, a fork runs live.

190
00:12:11,548 --> 00:12:16,681
So it means from call four onwards,
e-everything after that runs live, but

191
00:12:16,683 --> 00:12:23,572
anything before that is frozen and reused
from the, file that we saved earlier Okay,

192
00:12:24,204 --> 00:12:27,055
let's see it in action, in an actual demo.

193
00:12:27,363 --> 00:12:30,183
I recorded this run
earlier to save some time.

194
00:12:30,657 --> 00:12:35,053
I will show you where it went wrong,
replay it on my laptop, then fix

195
00:12:35,054 --> 00:12:36,904
the code and fork from call four.

196
00:12:38,170 --> 00:12:41,838
So before changing my screen,
here's the fix we'll make.

197
00:12:41,899 --> 00:12:46,689
So currently, the agent does send
the proposal result to the warehouse

198
00:12:46,716 --> 00:12:51,104
instead of the decision of the agent
to the warehouse, where the decision

199
00:12:51,104 --> 00:12:53,334
is the, correct value to be sent.

200
00:12:53,875 --> 00:12:59,468
So let me show you the, the entire thing
on my screen So I change my screen here.

201
00:13:00,337 --> 00:13:05,011
So yeah, as we can see, there is an
agent folder here and agent file.

202
00:13:05,427 --> 00:13:09,037
we don't really need to worry about
this, but, this is the, all the

203
00:13:09,053 --> 00:13:12,976
steps that we were talking earlier,
including the customer lookup,

204
00:13:12,983 --> 00:13:16,825
order lookup, the decision, the
act, and the reply to the customer.

205
00:13:17,016 --> 00:13:18,240
We don't really need to worry.

206
00:13:18,567 --> 00:13:23,840
And in order for us to record and
replay, we don't also need to, touch or

207
00:13:23,848 --> 00:13:26,531
change any of our existing agent code.

208
00:13:27,152 --> 00:13:33,856
In order to record, all we need
to do is, wrap, our LLMs and

209
00:13:33,856 --> 00:13:35,914
tools, with a few lines of code.

210
00:13:36,138 --> 00:13:41,396
In this case, I have a user lookup,
read, and a reserve, as a tool.

211
00:13:41,398 --> 00:13:45,420
So I wrap them once, and then
I wrap my LLM once as well.

212
00:13:45,666 --> 00:13:50,249
So in my agent, maybe an LLM or the
tools might be called multiple times.

213
00:13:50,588 --> 00:13:53,522
as long as we only wrap
them once, that's enough.

214
00:13:53,674 --> 00:13:57,852
So any calls that goes through them,
it will be recorded no matter how many

215
00:13:57,852 --> 00:13:59,700
times they are being called in the agent.

216
00:14:00,629 --> 00:14:05,481
so as I said, this, the failure run,
I recorded it earlier, and anytime an

217
00:14:05,488 --> 00:14:12,583
agent makes a run, it recorded in a, in
a file I call a cassette, s-such as this.

218
00:14:12,869 --> 00:14:17,859
And also there is a CLI that, we can
look that it shows, a little bit more.

219
00:14:18,453 --> 00:14:24,735
So if I write flashback LS, so
I can see all the cassettes.

220
00:14:24,749 --> 00:14:27,864
In this case, there is only one
cassette, which is a failure run,

221
00:14:28,259 --> 00:14:29,745
that we talk about in this slide.

222
00:14:29,941 --> 00:14:33,556
It, it have the six step
and the name and an ID.

223
